\label{p34}
    If the Gorenstein closure of $\mathbf R$ is a Bass order, then $\mathbf R$ is a ghost 
    intersection of maximal orders.

\label{p36}
    Let $\mathbf R$ be a Gorenstein order that is a ghost intersection of maximal orders. Then 
    $\mathbf R$ is a Bass order.

\label{e1}
    Let $L/K$ be an unramified quadratic extension. Let $\mathfrak{A}$ be a 
    quaternion division algebra, as before, so that $\mathcal{O}_\mathfrak{A}=
    \mathcal{O}_K[\mathbf{i},\mathbf{w}]$, where 
    $\mathbf{i}^2=\pi_K\mathbf{1}$, and
    $\mathbf{w}$ is a generator of the ring of integers of an unramified
    field $\mathfrak{L}\subseteq\mathfrak{A}$, on which conjugation by $i$
    induces the nontrivial element in the Galois group 
    $\mathrm{Gal}(\mathfrak{L}/K)$.
    Then $L\cong\mathfrak{L}$ as $K$-algebras, but we use different
    letter type to emphasize the particular roles each plays in the 
    computation. In fact, if $\omega\in L$ is the image of $\mathbf{w}$
    under a suitable isomorphism, then the algebra $\mathfrak{L}_L
    =L\otimes_K\mathfrak{L}$ has the idempotent
    $$\frac{1}{\bar{\omega}-\omega}\otimes\mathbf{w}-
    \frac{\omega}{\bar{\omega}-\omega}\otimes\mathbf{1},$$
    where $\bar{\omega}$ is the conjugate of $\omega$. It follows that
    $\mathfrak{L}_L$ is isomorphic to $L\times L$, and we can assume that
    the isomorphism takes $\mathbf{w}_L=1\otimes\mathbf{w}$ to $(\omega,\bar{\omega})$.
