    This implies that $\omega$ and $\bar{\omega}$ are the 
    eigenvalues of the matrix $\mathbf{w}_L$, and since they have 
    different images in the residue
    field, $\mathbf{w}_L$ is 
    contained in the maximal orders
    in an infinite path by 
    \cite[Prop. 4.2]{Eichler2}. By a similar argument,
    the matrix $\mathbf{i}_L$, 
    which generates the ring of integers
    of a ramified extension, is contained 
    in two maximal orders, and 
    conjugation by $\mathbf{i}_L$ transposes them. As conjugation by
    $\mathbf{i}_L$ also transposes the eigenvalues of 
    $\mathbf{w}_L$, and therefore the visual limits of the 
    corresponding path, it follows that the maximal orders containing
    $\mathbf{i}_L$ are the two maximal orders, $\mathbf{D}$ and $\mathbf{D}'$ 
    that are closest to the unique $\mathbf{i}_L$-invariant point 
    in the path, which is the barycenter of an edge, see Fig.\ref{f2}.

    Now, for each $n\geq 0$, let $\mathbf{D}_n$ be a maximal order in $\mathfrak{A}_L$
    whose corresponding vertex lies on the path at a distance of $n$ from 
    the vertex $\mathbf{D}=\mathbf{D}_0$, and at distance $n+1$ from $\mathbf{D}'$,
    as in Fig. \ref{f2}. The
    order $\mathbf{D}_n$ contains $\pi_K^n\mathbf{i}_L$, but not
    $\pi_K^{n-1}\mathbf{i}_L$. Since the orders of the form
    $\mathbf B_n=\mathcal{O}_K[\pi_K^n\mathbf{i},\mathbf{w}]$ are the only ones 
    containing $\mathcal{O}_{\mathfrak{L}}=\mathcal{O}_K[\mathbf{w}]$,
    by the preceding lemma, we have $\mathbf B_n=\mathfrak{A}\cap\mathbf{D}_n$.

\label{e2}
    Now assume the characteristic of $K$ is not $2$, and let
    $F=K[\sqrt{\pi_K}]$, which is a ramified quadratic extension.  As 
    before, $\mathfrak{A}$ is a division algebra, while $\mathfrak{A}_F$ is a matrix
    algebra where $$\mathtt{p}=\frac{1}{2\sqrt{\pi_K}}\otimes\mathbf{i}-
    \frac12\otimes\mathbf{1},$$ is an idempotent. In other words, 
    the algebra $\mathfrak{F}=K[\mathbf{i}]$ is a field isomorphic to $F$,
    while $\mathfrak{F}_F\cong F\times F$. Furthermore,
    $\mathcal{O}_F[\mathbf{w}_F]$ is the ring of integers
    of an unramified extension, whence it is contained in a unique
    maximal order $\mathbf{D}$. The same holds, therefore, for 
    $(\mathcal{O}_\mathfrak{A})_F$. Now the maximal orders containing 
    $\mathtt{p}$ lie in a maximal path $\mathfrak{p}$
    see Fig. \ref{f3nn}.
    As $\mathbf{i}'=\frac{1}{\sqrt{\pi_K}}\otimes\mathbf{i}=2\mathtt{p}+
    \mathbf{1}_L$ and $\mathbf{w}_L$ generate a cyclic
    algebra (and therefore a matrix algebra) over the residue field,
    they must generate a maximal order $\mathbf{D}$. We conclude that $\mathbf{D}$ 
    lies at a distance of $\nu_F(2)=2\nu_K(2)$ from $\mathfrak{p}$. Let 
    $\mathbf{D}'$ be the order of the line that is closest to $\mathbf{D}$. Note that 
    $\mathbf{D}=\mathbf{D}'$ when $K$ is not dyadic. Let $\mathbf{D}_n$ be an order at distance 
    $n$ from $\mathbf{D}$, either on the path from $\mathbf{D}$ to $\mathbf{D}'$ (when 
    $n\leq\nu_F(2)$), or in the path $\mathfrak{p}$ when $n\geq\nu_F(2)$).
    The order $\mathbf{D}_n$ contains $\sqrt{\pi_K}^n\mathbf{w}_L$
    but no $\sqrt{\pi_K}^{n-1}\mathbf{w}_L$. 
    However, all of them contain  $\mathbf{i}'$, which is a unit. We can 
    rephrase this as follows:
    \begin{itemize}
    \item When $n=2t$ then $\mathbf{D}_n$ contains
    $\pi_K^t\mathbf{w}_L=
    \mathbf{i}_L^n\mathbf{w}_L$,
    but not $\pi_K^{t-1}\mathbf{i}_L\mathbf{w}_L=
    \mathbf{i}_L^{n-1}\mathbf{w}_L$. 
    \item When $n=2t+1$ then $\mathbf{D}_n$ contains 
    $\pi_K^t\mathbf{i}_L\mathbf{w}_L=
    \mathbf{i}_L^n\mathbf{w}_L$, 
    but not $\pi_K^t\mathbf{w}_L=\mathbf{i}_L^{n-1}\mathbf{w}_L$. 
     \end{itemize}
    We conclude as before that $D_n\cap\mathfrak{A}=\mathcal{O}_K[\mathbf{i}^n\mathbf{w},\mathbf{i}]$,
    since all maximal orders containing $\mathbf{i}$ have this form.
